# SPK Kernels & Chebyshev — Deep Dive

```elixir
Mix.install(
  [
    {:eph_core, path: Path.expand("../..", __DIR__)},
    {:kino, "~> 0.14"},
    {:kino_vega_lite, "~> 0.1"}
  ],
  config: [
    eph_core: [
      # Planetary DE440s only. Avoid config_path: :eph_core here — that loads
      # config.exs asteroid kernels before SPK.Server starts.
      kernel_base_dir: Path.expand("../../priv", __DIR__),
      spk_kernels: ["de440s.bsp"]
    ]
  ]
)

Logger.configure(level: :warning)
```

## Overview

A deep dive for software engineers: how EphCore turns a NASA binary file into
"where is Mars right now?" — with ordinary Elixir, no NIFs.

### The metaphor (keep this in your head)

NASA does **not** ship a giant CSV of planet XYZ every second. That would be
enormous. Instead they ship **prebaked trajectory curves**: for each body, the
file is split into short time windows (~16 days for planets). Each window holds
a small list of numbers — think **animation keyframes / spline coefficients**,
not physics equations you have to derive at runtime.

Asking "where's Mars at this timestamp?" is the same shape as a cache lookup:

1. **Find the shard** — which time window covers this instant?
2. **Load the payload** — pull that window's numbers from an in-memory table (ETS).
3. **Evaluate the curve** — plug normalized time into a tiny polynomial formula.

That's the whole hot path. The rest of this notebook is just unpacking those
three steps with real code and real kernels.

**How to open:** Install [Livebook](https://livebook.dev/), open this `.livemd`,
evaluate the setup cell.

## Helpers

```elixir
alias VegaLite, as: Vl
alias EphCore.Ephemeris
alias EphCore.Ephemeris.Kernels.SPK
alias EphCore.Ephemeris.Kernels.SPK.{Chebyshev, DAF, Segment, Type2}
alias EphCore.AstronomicalTime.{JulianDay, TerrestrialTime}

:ok
```

## Ready check

```elixir
priv = EphCore.Ephemeris.Kernels.base_dir()
spk_path = Path.join([priv, "ephemeris", "spk", "de440s.bsp"])

Kino.Markdown.new("""
**EphCore** `#{Application.spec(:eph_core, :vsn)}` · priv `#{priv}`

SPK `de440s.bsp`: #{if File.exists?(spk_path), do: "✅ present", else: "❌ missing — run `mix eph.download_kernels`"}
""")
```

## 1 — Kernel health check

On boot, a GenServer opens the SPK file and indexes every **segment** — one
body-pair (e.g. Mars → solar-system barycenter) over a time span. Confirm the
server is up and peek at a few segments.

```elixir
segments = SPK.segments()

Kino.render(
  Kino.Markdown.new("""
SPK loaded? **#{SPK.loaded?()}** · Segment count: **#{length(segments)}**
""")
)

segments
|> Enum.take(5)
|> Enum.map(fn seg ->
  %{
    target: seg.target,
    center: seg.center,
    data_type: seg.data_type,
    start_epoch_s: seg.start_epoch,
    end_epoch_s: seg.end_epoch
  }
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new(name: "First 5 segments")
```

## 2 — What is a kernel?

An **SPK** (Spacecraft and Planet Kernel) is a binary blob from JPL. Under the
hood it's a **DAF** — think "custom file format with a header + linked index +
data pages," not a database you query with SQL. EphCore opens it with
`File.open` / `:file.pread` and unpacks records with Elixir binary pattern
matching (`<<>>`). No C NIFs, no ports.

```mermaid
graph TD
    A["de440s.bsp on disk"] -->|"File.open :binary"| B[DAF header record]
    B --> C[Summary records chain]
    C --> D[Segment descriptors]
    D --> E["Type 2 curve data arrays"]
    B -->|"binary header match"| F[Endian detection]
    F --> C
```

**Key insight:** `DAF` is the deserializer. It reads the header, walks a linked
list of summary records (the index), and hands back segment descriptors — like
parsing a custom binary protocol with `<<>>` on random-access chunks.

```elixir
{:ok, daf} = DAF.parse(spk_path)

Kino.Markdown.new("""
### DAF header fields

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| nd (doubles per summary) | `#{daf.nd}` |
| ni (integers per summary) | `#{daf.ni}` |
| endian | `#{daf.endian}` |
| summary count | `#{length(daf.summaries)}` |
""")
```

Each summary packs `nd` doubles (start/end epoch) and `ni` integers
(target, center, frame, data_type, start_addr, end_addr) — the index
entry that tells us where that body's curve data lives in the file.

## 3 — From body name to geocentric position

Friendly atoms like `:mars` map to **NAIF IDs** (JPL's integer body codes —
same idea as an enum / foreign key). We look up Mars and Earth in the solar
system's reference frame (SSB = "origin of the coordinate system"), then
subtract to get a vector from Earth to Mars.

```mermaid
graph LR
    A[":mars"] -->|"NAIF map"| B["4 (Mars barycenter)"]
    B -->|"SPK.position_at(4, 0, s)"| C["Mars SSB (km)"]
    D["Earth SSB (km)"] -->|subtract| E["Geocentric Mars (km)"]
    C --> E
```

**NAIF ID map** (from `EphCore.Ephemeris`):

```elixir
naif_ids = %{
  sun: 10, moon: 301,
  mercury: 1, venus: 2, mars: 4,
  jupiter: 5, saturn: 6, uranus: 7, neptune: 8, pluto: 9,
  earth: 399, emb: 3, ssb: 0
}

Kino.DataTable.new(
  Enum.map(naif_ids, fn {name, id} -> %{body: name, naif_id: id} end),
  name: "NAIF IDs (DE440s)"
)
```

Now let's call the full geocentric pipeline:

```elixir
utc = ~U[2026-07-21 17:00:00Z]
tt = TerrestrialTime.from_utc(utc)
jd_tt = JulianDay.from_datetime(tt)

{:ok, mars} = Ephemeris.geocentric_state(jd_tt, :mars)

Kino.Markdown.new("""
### Mars geocentric state at #{DateTime.to_iso8601(utc)} UTC

| Component | Value |
|-----------|-------|
| X (km) | `#{elem(mars.geocentric_position_km, 0) |> Float.round(3)}` |
| Y (km) | `#{elem(mars.geocentric_position_km, 1) |> Float.round(3)}` |
| Z (km) | `#{elem(mars.geocentric_position_km, 2) |> Float.round(3)}` |
| Range (km) | `#{Float.round(mars.geocentric_range_km, 3)}` |
| Range (AU) | `#{Float.round(mars.geocentric_range_km / 149_597_870.7, 6)}` |
| Frame | `#{mars.frame}` |
| Seconds past J2000 | `#{Float.round(mars.query_seconds_past_j2000, 3)}` |
""")
```

## 4 — Inside `SPK.position_at/3`

What happens when you call `SPK.position_at(4, 0, seconds)`?
Remember the metaphor: **find the shard → load the payload → evaluate the curve.**

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant Caller
    participant ETS
    participant Type2
    participant Curve

    Caller->>ETS: lookup meta for body pair
    ETS-->>Caller: segment metadata list
    Caller->>Caller: pick segment covering this time
    Caller->>Type2: find_record_index
    Type2-->>Caller: window index
    Caller->>ETS: lookup record for that window
    ETS-->>Caller: mid, radius, X/Y/Z coeffs
    Caller->>Curve: normalize time into window
    Curve-->>Caller: t in -1..1
    Caller->>Curve: evaluate X, Y, Z
    Curve-->>Caller: position km
```

**ETS layout** (written once at load, read-only afterward — like a warm cache):

* `{:meta, target, center}` → "which windows exist for this body pair?"
* `{:record, target, center, index}` → "the curve numbers for window N"

Coefficients are **pre-reversed** at load time so the hot path doesn't pay for
`Enum.reverse/1` on every query — reverse once, evaluate many times.

```elixir
# Peek at the raw ETS meta entry for Mars (target=4, center=0)
[{_key, metas}] = :ets.lookup(EphCore.Ephemeris.Kernels.SPK.Server, {:meta, 4, 0})
meta = hd(metas)

type2 = meta.type2

Kino.Markdown.new("""
### Mars (4→0) Type 2 directory — the shard index

| Field | Value | Meaning |
|-------|-------|---------|
| init | `#{type2.init}` | First window start (s past J2000) |
| intlen | `#{type2.intlen}` | Window length (s) |
| rsize | `#{type2.rsize}` | Doubles stored per window |
| n | `#{type2.n}` | How many windows |
| coeff_count | `#{div(type2.rsize - 2, 3)}` | Numbers per axis in each window |
| span (years) | `#{Float.round(type2.n * type2.intlen / 86400 / 365.25, 1)}` | Total coverage |
""")
```

## 5 — Evaluating the curve (Chebyshev, without the headache)

### What are we actually computing?

Each window stores a short list of coefficients for X, Y, and Z. Evaluating the
position is: **take those numbers + a time in [-1, 1], get a point on the curve.**

Under the hood JPL uses Chebyshev polynomials (a particular basis that packs
smooth paths efficiently — like choosing a good compression codec). You don't
need the math identity sheet; treat it as "polynomial evaluation with a known
fast algorithm."

If you want the textbook form:

$$
x(t) = \sum_{k=0}^{N} c_k \cdot T_k(t), \quad t \in [-1, 1]
$$

### Step 1: Normalize time into the window

Each record has a `mid` (center of the window) and `radius` (half-width). Map
your absolute timestamp into local coordinates on [-1, 1] — same idea as
normalizing a cache key into a shard's local offset:

$$
t = \frac{\text{epoch} - \text{mid}}{\text{radius}}
$$

```elixir
j2000_jd = 2_451_545.0
seconds = (jd_tt - j2000_jd) * 86_400.0

index = Type2.find_record_index(type2, seconds)
[{_key, {mid, radius, cx, cy, cz}}] =
  :ets.lookup(EphCore.Ephemeris.Kernels.SPK.Server, {:record, 4, 0, index})

t = Chebyshev.normalize_time(seconds, mid, radius)

Kino.Markdown.new("""
### Normalization for Mars at epoch

| | Value |
|--|-------|
| epoch (s past J2000) | `#{Float.round(seconds, 3)}` |
| record index | `#{index}` |
| mid | `#{Float.round(mid, 3)}` |
| radius | `#{Float.round(radius, 3)}` s = `#{Float.round(radius / 86400, 2)}` days |
| **t** | `#{Float.round(t, 8)}` ∈ [-1, 1] ✓ |
""")
```

### Step 2: Clenshaw — the fast evaluator (the hot path)

Naively building each basis term and summing is quadratic. **Clenshaw** is the
standard linear-time trick for this family of polynomials — cousin to Horner's
method for ordinary polynomials. One tight loop, no intermediate arrays:

```
b_{N+1} = 0,  b_{N+2} = 0
b_k = 2t · b_{k+1} - b_{k+2} + c_k     (for k = N, N-1, ..., 0)
result = b_0 - t · b_1
```

In EphCore that's a **tail-recursive** function over pre-reversed coefficients:

```elixir
# The evaluate_reversed/2 function in action (coefficients are already high→low)
x = Chebyshev.evaluate_reversed(cx, t)
y = Chebyshev.evaluate_reversed(cy, t)
z = Chebyshev.evaluate_reversed(cz, t)

Kino.Markdown.new("""
### Evaluated position (km, ICRF)

| Axis | Value |
|------|-------|
| X | `#{Float.round(x, 6)}` |
| Y | `#{Float.round(y, 6)}` |
| Z | `#{Float.round(z, 6)}` |
| Coefficients per axis | `#{length(cx)}` (degree #{length(cx) - 1}) |
""")
```

### Toy example: a hand-made curve

Forget planets for a second. A 4-number payload `[2, -3, 0.5, 1]` defines a
smooth function on [-1, 1]. Same API we'll use for Mars — just smaller.

```elixir
coeffs = [2.0, -3.0, 0.5, 1.0]

points =
  for i <- 0..100 do
    t_val = -1.0 + i * 0.02
    %{t: t_val, f: Chebyshev.evaluate(coeffs, t_val)}
  end

Vl.new(width: 520, height: 260, title: "Toy Chebyshev series: 2T₀ − 3T₁ + 0.5T₂ + T₃")
|> Vl.data_from_values(points)
|> Vl.mark(:line, color: "#6366f1")
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "t", type: :quantitative, title: "t ∈ [-1, 1]")
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "f", type: :quantitative, title: "f(t)")
```

### Visualize Mars X-coefficient magnitudes

Higher-degree terms get tiny fast — that's why a short coefficient list still
hits sub-km accuracy over a multi-day window. Same reason JPEG can drop high
frequencies: most of the signal lives in the early terms.

```elixir
# cx is pre-reversed (high→low), so reverse back to get natural order
cx_natural = Enum.reverse(cx)

coeff_data =
  cx_natural
  |> Enum.with_index()
  |> Enum.map(fn {c, i} -> %{degree: i, magnitude: abs(c), log_mag: :math.log10(max(abs(c), 1.0e-30))} end)

Vl.new(width: 520, height: 260, title: "Mars X-axis Chebyshev coefficient magnitudes (log₁₀)")
|> Vl.data_from_values(coeff_data)
|> Vl.mark(:bar, color: "#f59e0b")
|> Vl.encode_field(:x, "degree", type: :ordinal, title: "Degree k")
|> Vl.encode_field(:y, "log_mag", type: :quantitative, title: "log₁₀|cₖ|")
```

## 6 — Raw Mars query end-to-end

Wire it together: wall-clock UTC → internal time scales → seconds past J2000 →
`SPK.position_at` (the shard → payload → curve path from §4).

```elixir
# Full manual pipeline
utc_now = ~U[2026-07-21 17:00:00Z]
tt_now = TerrestrialTime.from_utc(utc_now)
jd_tt_now = JulianDay.from_datetime(tt_now)
j2000 = 2_451_545.0
secs = (jd_tt_now - j2000) * 86_400.0

{us, {:ok, pos}} = :timer.tc(fn -> SPK.position_at(4, 0, secs) end)

{x, y, z} = pos
range = :math.sqrt(x * x + y * y + z * z)

Kino.Markdown.new("""
### `SPK.position_at(4, 0, #{Float.round(secs, 1)})` — Mars SSB

| | Value |
|--|-------|
| X | `#{Float.round(x, 3)}` km |
| Y | `#{Float.round(y, 3)}` km |
| Z | `#{Float.round(z, 3)}` km |
| Range from SSB | `#{Float.round(range, 3)}` km |
| Query time | **#{us} µs** |

*Compare: the geocentric state above subtracts Earth's position, giving a
different vector and range. This cell is the raw "from solar-system origin"
position.*
""")
```

### Benchmark: 10 000 queries

Tip: in Livebook, convert this subsection's parent section to a **branching
section** if you want to skip the bench during dry-runs.

```elixir
epochs = for i <- 0..9999, do: secs + i * 86_400.0 / 10

{bench_us, _} =
  :timer.tc(fn ->
    Enum.each(epochs, fn e -> SPK.position_at_fast(4, 0, e) end)
  end)

Kino.Markdown.new("""
**10 000 queries** in #{Float.round(bench_us / 1000, 1)} ms
→ **#{Float.round(bench_us / 10_000, 2)} µs/query** (ETS lookup + curve eval, no telemetry)
""")
```

## 7 — Type 21 / Difference Arrays (asteroids)

Same container format (SPK/DAF), **different codec** for the curve payload.

* Planets in DE440s use **Type 2** — fixed-length Chebyshev windows (what we
  walked through above).
* Asteroids from Horizons (e.g. `2000001.bsp` for Ceres) use **Type 21** —
  difference-array records (a different interpolation scheme from SPICELIB).

Think JPEG vs PNG: same "image file" idea, different bytes on disk, different
decoder. The public API still answers "where is this body at time T?"

```mermaid
graph TD
    subgraph planets["Type 2 - planets"]
        A[Fixed-length curve windows] --> B["Clenshaw - linear in N"]
    end
    subgraph asteroids["Type 21 - asteroids"]
        C[Variable-order difference records] --> D[Difference-array decoder]
        D --> E[Position and velocity]
    end
```

**Key differences:**

|             | Type 2 (planets)           | Type 21 (asteroids)          |
| ----------- | -------------------------- | ---------------------------- |
| Source      | JPL DE series              | JPL Horizons                 |
| Bodies      | Planets, Sun, Moon         | Small bodies                 |
| Decoder     | Clenshaw over coefficients | Difference-array walk        |
| Storage     | Fixed coeffs per axis      | Difference table + stepsizes |
| Our modules | `Type2` + `Chebyshev`      | `Type21` + `DifferenceArray` |

`DifferenceArray` is a pure-Elixir port of the Fortran decoder. Asteroid
kernels live under `priv/ephemeris/spk/asteroids/` — not loaded here to keep
this notebook light.

```elixir
# Show which data types are present in our loaded segments
segments
|> Enum.frequencies_by(& &1.data_type)
|> Enum.map(fn {type, count} ->
  label = case type do
    2 -> "Type 2 (Chebyshev)"
    21 -> "Type 21 (MDA)"
    other -> "Type #{other}"
  end
  %{data_type: type, label: label, segment_count: count}
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new(name: "Loaded segment types")
```

## 8 — Why Elixir? (talk beats)

```mermaid
mindmap
  root((EphCore))
    Binary matching
      Unpack floats in Elixir
      No memcpy, no NIF
      DAF header in a few lines
    ETS read concurrency
      Named public table
      Concurrent reads scale
      Thousands of queries per ms
    Pre-reversed coefficients
      Reverse once at load
      Skip reverse on hot path
      About 15 percent faster
    Fast curve eval
      Linear cost per axis
      Tail recursive loop
      No temp basis arrays
    No GIL
      Fan-out with Task.async_stream
      Scales across cores
      Full-year almanac fan-out
```

### Summary of the architecture

Same three-step metaphor, with BEAM-shaped implementation details:

1. **Load time (warm the cache)** — Parse the binary index, then stuff every
   window's curve numbers into ETS. Reverse coefficients once so queries stay
   cheap.

2. **Query time (shard → payload → curve)** — One ETS read for metadata, integer
   math to pick the window, one ETS read for coefficients, normalize time,
   evaluate. Two lookups + arithmetic. That's it.

3. **Concurrency** — ETS with `read_concurrency: true` is shared memory without
   a mutex tax on readers. An almanac job can `Task.async_stream` 365 days × 10
   bodies across cores with no SPK coordination layer.

4. **No GIL, no FFI** — The decoder and evaluator are plain Elixir on the BEAM.
   Boot opens the file; after that it's process-local work. Deploy with
   `mix release` — no native toolchain, no linked astronomy library.

